    "Route-set evidence only: the oracle is the framework route table read at import time. Nothing in this record issued an HTTP request, so no entry is Runtime-tested.",
    "HEAD and OPTIONS are dropped on both sides. A scanner pair whose method is null compares as \"-\" and is reported as unresolved when the oracle has a real method for that path, never as an exact match.",
    "An outcome of flagged means the named flag is present in the layer output for that fixture and the covers text says why it applies; it is not proof the layer understood the construct. A flag that also fires on exact fixtures is not counted as covering unless the missed construct is the one it names.",
    "CI does not reinstall the framework: the stored oracle output is bound by sha256, by the pin and by the sha256 of oracle.py. Only the in-repo static scanners are re-run live, which needs python3 on PATH (a missing python3 fails the verification, it is never skipped).",
    "Only the pinned version was run. Releases uploaded after the pinned wheel are listed per package and are not covered.",
    "record_digest seals the whole record, so an edit that is not re-sealed fails. Prose is sealed, not recomputed: statement, reasons, limits, plan, oracle_range text, layer roles, construct and covers texts and the stdout_unstored text are not derived from evidence, so a re-sealed prose edit is not detected by the verifier and must be caught in review.",
    "A Django URLConf declares no HTTP methods, so every pair uses method \"-\" on both sides; per-method behaviour is not covered by this record.",
    "The T12 leaf emits no routes for any input; its covering flags are the profile-level blanket unknown or the forwarded T06 unknown, so a flagged outcome for the leaf says \"blocked\", not \"understood\".",
    "oracle.py groups django.contrib.admin, django.views.static (the static() helper), django.contrib.staticfiles and rest_framework callbacks as generated (the admin paths in generated-admin); no fixture uses static() or staticfiles. Other views Django ships (the auth URLs, flatpages, sitemaps, admindocs, generic template views) are reported as ordinary routes, which holds a scanner to the stricter side. Routes a DRF router expands from a user ViewSet are ordinary routes in the oracle (their callbacks carry the user module).",
    "Pins follow the T12 profile versionPin.framework string (checked against pin.packages by the verifier); later releases exist and are listed as not covered.",
    "A caught or flagged outcome of a catch-or-flag entry covers the routes the URLConf declares. The admin URLs are reported as generated by oracle.py and are assessed only under the generated-admin entry, so a supported ordinary-route entry says nothing about them.",
    "Held to shape, order and the hash of the file, not recomputed from a second source: the values the comparisons do not read (in the registry extract the info_version, requires_python, size, upload time and yanked flag; in an oracle row the kind, name, view, regex and viewset_actions; in a generated group its name; in pip freeze the packages besides the pinned ones and the order of the lines), a method every comparison drops (HEAD, OPTIONS) and the path of a generated route that has no other method. The PyPI response is not stored, so a registry command states no hash and no size. A set of method and path pairs collapses a pair that two different routes register. The members of a SCOPE.json object may be reordered without changing the seal (it sorts keys); the order of the must-catch, limits and plan lists is sealed but is the author's."
